ef "/venues/133"} "Utah Olympic Oval, Kearns, Utah"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/1114"} "16 from 10 countries"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record "] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/1100"} "13:15.33"] " " [:span {:class "label label-primary"} "WR"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/99378"} "Gianni Romme"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"] " / 17 February 1998 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/534"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Starter"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002112"} "Franco Ceschi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003128"} "Knut Ludahl"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Dutch skaters were highly favored in this event. Having swept the medals at the 1998 Olympics, they had finished 1-2 at all World Championships since. Three of them had won the world title: 1998 Olympic Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/99378"} "Gianni Romme"] " (1998, 2000), 1998 silver medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/99379"} "Bob de Jong"] " (1999) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/100954"} "Carl Verheijen"] " (2001). Reigning World Champion Verheijen had not made the Dutch team, however, but with " [:a {:href "/athletes/100951"} "Jochem Uytdehaage"] ", winner of the Olympic 5,000 m, he had a decent replacement. The best non-Dutch prospect was " [:a {:href "/athletes/85221"} "Frank Dittrich"] ". The German had been bronze medallist at the World Championships 1998-2000 and had won the only World Cup competition at the distance earlier that season, when none of the Dutch skaters even made the podium."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Defending champion Romme had only narrowly qualified at the Dutch trials, suffering from illness at the time. The first of the favorites to skate in Salt Lake City, he set out to break his world record of 13:03.40, which he had set in late 2000. Still not in his 1998 form, he set out fast, as usual, but faded sooner than expected. His 13:10.03 was still one of the fastest ever 10,000 m recorded. Having won two Olympic medals already, much was expected from Jochem Uytdehaage. The Dutchman set out for a very tight schedule, recording lap times between 30.64 and 31.48. Not only did this result in a new world record, his time of 12:58.92 also made him the first man to beat 13 minutes in the 10,000 m."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With these results, the first two places seemed secure, and the bronze was to be decided in the very last pair. Frank Dittrich, known for his fast final laps, was paired with " [:a {:href "/athletes/99381"} "Lasse Sætre"] " of Norway, known for his ability to )die and resurrect) during a race. The two remained close at first, with Sætre pulling away around the half-way point. While the German’s final sprint did not come, Sætre skated to a new personal best and a bronze medal."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button684649", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button684649').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "400 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "800 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1600 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "2000 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :clas